name: Canvases
order: 141
------------

To display output, *Collider.JAM* needs rendering surfaces.
We use one or multiple HTML5 canvases for this purpose.
Usually, there is no need to specify them manually,
*Collider.JAM* creates and manages canvases automatically.
By default, *Collider.JAM* setups two canvases - 
one for the Canvas API and another for WebGL context.
Both are visible at the same time with the 2D canvas
located as an overlay in front of the WebGL canvas.


=== Canvas API and WebGL Dichotomy

A canvas is a versatile HTML5 element that allows us to draw
graphics on a web page.
It provides an immediate-mode API to define the rendering context
(including coordinate system, line width, current color...)
and send drawing commands.

There are multiple graphics APIs available for the canvas components.
We can select them by requesting a corresponding drawing context
from the canvas element.

When "2d" context is requested, we are getting access
to the 2D Canvas API.

When "webgl2" context is requested, we can use WebGL2 API.
There are also legacy "webgl-experimental" and "webgl"
we don't use since currently WebGL2 is widely supported
in modern browsers. There is also a perspective "webgpu"
that promises much better performance than WebGL,
but it is still a candidate recommendation and not a standard.
We prefer to work with super-stable, well-documented and
deeply understood Canvas and WebGL APIs, leaving WebGPU
integration for the future.

=== Why Two APIs?

One might ask why do we have to bother with 2 different API?
Just pick one and move on.

Unfortunatelly, there is no particular winner.
Both Canvas 2D API and WebGL are good and flexible APIs
with decent performance and hardware acceleration.

We prefer Canvas 2D API for vercatile vector and bitmap graphics.
It provides a rich set of 2D drawing operations and includes out-of-the-box
support for advanced features like vector fonts and SVG rendering.
Something, that is completely missing in the WebGL API.

WebGL, on the other hand, is a much more low-level API
that gives you finer control over the rendering pipeline.
It is much preferable when we deal with 3D graphics, post-processing effects
and anywhere where performace could be an issue.

*Collider.JAM* provides convenient wrappers and tools to streamline both
APIs usage. But doesn't fully unify them since usage patterns for both APIs
are totally different and unification might create unnecessary bottlenecks
with little advantage.


